# SRG-APP-000360-AU-000130  -  CAT III
# For the host and devices within its scope of coverage, the Central Log
# Server must be configured to send a real-time alert to the System
# Administrator (SA) and Information System Security Officer (ISSO) (at a
# minimum) of all audit failure events, such as loss of communications with
# hosts and devices, or if log records are no longer being received.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000360-AU-000130
group:           V-206493
severity:        low
cat:             CAT III
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-001858
control_rev5:    AU-5

contribution:    Splunk carries this with its own reporting and notification tools, which
                 is what the product STIG directs: a scheduled search over the audit data
                 with an alert condition and an email recipient list, and the health
                 reporter's alerting wired to an action.

governs:         health.conf - Thresholds for Splunk reporting on its own health.
governs:         savedsearches.conf - Saved searches, scheduled reports and alerts.

check:           savedsearches.conf [<stanza name>] search non_empty
check:           savedsearches.conf [<stanza name>] action.email.to non_empty
check:           savedsearches.conf [<stanza name>] enableSched equals 1
check:           health.conf [health_reporter] alert.disabled equals 0
check:           health.conf [health_reporter] alert.actions non_empty
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          savedsearches.conf [<stanza name>] alert_condition https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/savedsearches.conf
vendor:          savedsearches.conf [<stanza name>] search https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/savedsearches.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        SMTP relay reachability and delivery within seconds, which depends on the
                 mail server and the network rather than on Splunk
external:        The SA and ISSO recipient addresses, which the organization supplies
external:        Which hosts and devices are within the scope of coverage, and the ISSO's
                 confirmation of receipt that SPLK-CL-000170 obtains by interview
external:        Which saved search carries the trigger, which is organization-defined
                 content: a trigger is expressed either by alert_condition or by counttype
                 with relation and quantity, so a test on alert_condition alone reports a
                 finding against a search that is correctly configured the other way.

citation:        Two corrections. savedsearches.conf realtime_schedule in 1|true is both
                 vacuous and a misreading: savedsearches.conf.spec:137-155 defaults it to
                 true and describes it as how the scheduler computes the next run time, not
                 whether the alert is real time, and spec:151 directs continuous scheduling
                 (false) whenever summary indexing is enabled - so the condition could fail
                 a legitimate configuration while never catching an alert that is not
                 scheduled at all. enableSched, which defaults to 0 (spec:73-77), is the
                 setting that decides that. health.conf [feature:*] alert.actions non_empty
                 is outside the feature stanza's vocabulary: alert.actions is documented
                 only under [health_reporter] (health.conf.spec:93) and the shipped file
                 sets it there (health.conf:27), so the condition failed every install;
                 read over [feature:*] it also demanded an action on the three dozen
                 shipped features that have nothing to do with audit failure, two of which
                 ship with alerting disabled (health.conf:74, 83).
finding:         The application has no enabled, scheduled alert with a recipient list over
                 audit failure events, or health reporter alerting is turned off, so loss
                 of communications or a halt in received records raises no notification.
